{
  "id": "Compound::DB00937 - Gene::6530",
  "verbalization": "Diethylpropion is implicated in the pathogenesis of psychotic episodes and increased sympathetic activity, both of which are associated with the drug's effects on the central nervous system. These pathways converge on Slc6a2, a gene encoding a sodium transporter that plays a role in neurotransmitter regulation and neuronal function. The evidence suggests that Diethylpropion may influence Slc6a2 through a mechanism involving the modulation of neurotransmitter activity, particularly in the context of stimulant effects and psychotic symptoms. The shared lowest common ancestors between Diethylpropion and Methamphetamine, as well as Phendimetrazine, indicate a potential role in the broader network of central nervous system signaling. The observed binding of Methamphetamine and Phendimetrazine to Slc6a2 supports the hypothesis that Diethylpropion may exert its effects via a similar mechanism, possibly by modulating the activity of Slc6a2 in the brain. This integrated evidence suggests a plausible, mechanistically grounded relationship between Diethylpropion and Slc6a2, though further experimental validation is required to confirm the exact nature of the interaction.",
